window.STUDY_DATA = {"chapters":[{"id":1,"title":"Foundations of project management","week":"Week 1 · Sep 9","pages":"1–46","pdfPages":"26–71","notes":["Separate projects (temporary, unique outcomes) from recurring operations. Temporary refers to a finite endeavour, not a short-lived product.","Balance scope, time, and cost, but also consider quality, risk, resources, stakeholder satisfaction, and business value.","A program coordinates related projects for combined benefits. A portfolio selects and balances investments against organizational strategy.","Know the ten knowledge areas: integration, scope, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement, and stakeholder management.","Distinguish the sponsor’s backing and resources from the project manager’s coordination. Practice classifying situations, not just memorizing labels.","Organizational project management connects project, program, and portfolio management to strategy. PMI’s ninth-edition talent triangle: technical project management, leadership, strategic and business management.","A servant leader emphasizes relationships and service; leadership style should fit the situation. PMI’s ethical values include responsibility, respect, fairness, and honesty. Software supports judgment; it does not replace it."]},{"id":2,"title":"The IT project context","week":"Week 2 · Sep 16","pages":"47–84","pdfPages":"72–109","notes":["Use a systems view: the three spheres are business, organization, and technology. A technically successful solution can still fail organizationally.","Four organizational frames: structural (roles), human resources (people’s needs), political (power and conflict), symbolic (meaning and culture).","Functional structures give project managers limited authority; matrix structures share authority; pure project structures give them more.","Project phases are not the five process groups. Product life cycles can outlast the projects that create products. Phase gates may continue, redirect, or stop work.","Agile values responsiveness, collaboration, working software, and interaction without eliminating useful documentation or planning. Virtual teams need deliberate trust and communication.","Systems philosophy: holistic view. Systems analysis: solve problems by examining components and alternatives. Systems management: address business, organizational, and technological issues.","Weak, balanced, and strong matrix structures differ. Moderate-to-high PM authority and PM budget control indicate a strong matrix.","Iteration refines; increments add functionality. Hybrid approaches combine approaches. Outsourcing means an external provider; offshoring means another country."]},{"id":3,"title":"Process groups & agile","week":"Week 2 · Sep 16","pages":"85–150","pdfPages":"110–175","notes":["Five process groups: initiating, planning, executing, monitoring and controlling, closing. They overlap and can recur within phases.","A business case justifies an investment; a charter authorizes it. A kick-off aligns participants; it does not replace authorization.","Executing usually consumes the most resources. Monitoring and controlling compares actual performance to plans while work continues.","Product owner: product value and backlog priorities. Scrum master: facilitate Scrum and remove impediments. Developers: organize delivery work.","Product backlog covers product needs; sprint backlog covers selected sprint work. Review inspects the product; retrospective improves the process; burndown shows remaining work.","Table 3-1 maps the processes to knowledge areas. Initiating: Develop Project Charter and Identify Stakeholders. Closing: Close Project or Phase. Estimate Activity Resources is in resource management, not schedule management, in this edition.","JWD predictive uses one software delivery; its agile variant uses three releases and four-week sprints. Both retain business purpose and coordination. A team charter describes working agreements; a project charter authorizes the project."]},{"id":4,"title":"Integration management","week":"Week 3 · Sep 23","pages":"151–200","pdfPages":"176–225","notes":["Integration reconciles cross-area trade-offs. Strategic fit should inform project selection before technical enthusiasm drives the decision.","NPV = sum of discounted net cash flows, including the initial outflow. For rate r and period t, divide that period’s cash flow by (1+r)^t.","Simple ROI = (benefits − costs) / costs. Simple payback is when cumulative net inflows recover the investment. Neither substitutes for every strategic criterion.","Weighted score = sum of weight × criterion score. Weights normally sum to 100%; use consistent scales and direction.","The charter authorizes; the management plan coordinates action. Evaluate requested changes, follow agreed approval authority, and communicate approved updates.","Capture lessons throughout delivery. Tacit knowledge often needs interaction; explicit knowledge is readily documented. Cancelled projects still require proper closure.","The integration list and Figure 4-1 contain seven processes, including Manage Project Knowledge, despite nearby prose saying six.","Corrective action realigns performance; preventive action reduces future negative consequences; defect repair fixes nonconforming results. Configuration management identifies and controls product characteristics and versions."]},{"id":5,"title":"Scope management","week":"Week 4 · Sep 30","pages":"201–240","pdfPages":"226–265","notes":["Product scope describes features; project scope describes all work needed to produce and deliver the outcome.","Scope processes: plan scope management, collect requirements, define scope, create WBS, validate scope, control scope.","Requirements traceability connects requirements to sources and downstream deliverables or tests. Prototypes help clarify unclear needs.","WBS: deliverable-oriented decomposition of all approved work. Work packages are the lowest level; the WBS dictionary explains components in detail.","Scope baseline = approved scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary. A WBS is not an activity dependency network.","Quality control checks correctness; scope validation obtains formal acceptance. Scope creep is uncontrolled expansion; useful-looking extras still need approval.","A work package can have one accountable person and several contributors. Include management work in the WBS and avoid overlap or omissions.","Validate Scope obtains acceptance; Control Scope manages status and changes. Requirements management planning explains how requirements will be documented, prioritized, and tracked."]},{"id":6,"title":"Schedule management","week":"Week 5 · Oct 7","pages":"241–284","pdfPages":"266–309","notes":["Duration is elapsed working time; effort is labor. An activity requiring 16 hours at 4 hours per day has 4 working days of duration.","A milestone is a significant event with zero duration. Mandatory, discretionary, and external describe why dependencies exist.","Finish-to-start, start-to-start, finish-to-finish, and start-to-finish describe relationship types. Lead allows overlap; lag imposes waiting.","Critical path = longest duration path through the network. It controls earliest finish under the model’s assumptions and can change as work changes.","Total float = late start − early start = late finish − early finish. Free float concerns delay without affecting the early start of a successor.","Crashing seeks schedule compression for the least incremental cost, often by adding resources; fast tracking overlaps sequential work. Both have trade-offs. Critical chain includes resource constraints and buffers.","PERT expected duration = (optimistic + 4 × most likely + pessimistic) / 6. Do not substitute the simple three-point average.","Six schedule processes: Plan Schedule Management, Define Activities, Sequence Activities, Estimate Activity Durations, Develop Schedule, Control Schedule.","Forward pass at a merge: use the latest predecessor early finish. Backward pass at a split: use the earliest successor late start. These simple rules assume zero-lag finish-to-start links.","An activity can have total float but zero free float. Multiple critical paths must all be addressed to shorten completion. Critical chain uses project and feeding buffers and addresses resource constraints."]},{"id":7,"title":"Cost management","week":"Week 8 · Oct 28","pages":"285–326","pdfPages":"310–351","notes":["Compare future relevant costs and benefits; do not continue solely because of sunk costs. Life cycle costing includes ownership beyond acquisition.","Analogous uses similar prior work; parametric applies a rate/model; bottom-up aggregates detailed estimates.","The cost baseline is time-phased. Contingency reserves address identified risks; management reserves address unforeseen work and are outside the baseline.","PV = planned value; EV = budgeted value of work performed; AC = actual cost. CV = EV − AC; SV = EV − PV. Negative variances are unfavorable.","CPI = EV / AC; SPI = EV / PV. Values below 1 are unfavorable. EAC = BAC / CPI when current cost efficiency is assumed to continue.","EVM schedule variance is expressed in value units, not directly in days. A favorable cost indicator does not prove favorable schedule performance."]},{"id":8,"title":"Quality management","week":"Week 8 · Oct 28","pages":"327–372","pdfPages":"352–397","notes":["Quality means meeting requirements and being fit for use. Grade or feature count is not the same as quality.","Plan quality sets standards and methods; manage quality improves processes; control quality checks specific outputs.","Pareto: rank major contributors. Cause-and-effect: potential causes. Control chart: variation over time. Scatter: relationship between variables.","Also know checksheets, histograms, and flowcharts. Statistical sampling evaluates part of a population; correlation does not establish causation.","DMAIC = define, measure, analyze, improve, control. Six Sigma emphasizes reducing defects and variation.","Prevention avoids defects; appraisal evaluates conformance; internal failure is before delivery; external failure is after delivery.","Unit testing checks components; integration checks interfaces; system testing checks the whole system; user acceptance tests focus on user acceptance."]},{"id":9,"title":"Resource management","week":"Week 9 · Nov 4","pages":"373–424","pdfPages":"398–449","notes":["Maslow: physiological, safety, social, esteem, self-actualization. Herzberg: hygiene reduces dissatisfaction; achievement and growth can motivate.","McClelland: achievement, affiliation, power. Theory X assumes control is needed; Theory Y supports self-direction and responsibility.","Power bases: legitimate (position), reward, coercive, expert (knowledge), referent (admiration).","RACI = responsible, accountable, consulted, informed. Clarify who performs work and who owns the outcome.","Resource leveling resolves over-allocation and can extend duration. Acquiring obtains resources; developing improves skills and team effectiveness.","Tuckman: forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning. Collaboration seeks a joint solution; compromise gives up something on both sides.","Emotional intelligence includes awareness and regulation of emotions. Physical resources also fall within the ninth edition’s resource-management scope."]},{"id":10,"title":"Communications management","week":"Week 9 · Nov 4","pages":"425–462","pdfPages":"450–487","notes":["Plan communications around information needs, recipients, timing, methods, ownership, and feedback. More detail is not always better.","Potential two-way communication channels = n(n−1)/2. To find added channels, calculate both totals and subtract.","Interactive is two-way exchange; push sends to recipients; pull makes information available for retrieval. Sending does not prove understanding.","Status reports describe the current position; progress reports describe work completed over a period; forecasts predict future outcomes.","Communicate bad news promptly with evidence and options. Listen actively and confirm meaning before assuming agreement.","Use purposeful meetings with agendas, decisions, and assigned actions. Monitor whether communication actually meets needs and adjust accordingly."]},{"id":11,"title":"Risk management","week":"Week 10 · Nov 11","pages":"463–504","pdfPages":"488–529","notes":["Risks are uncertain threats or opportunities. Current problems belong in issue management; risk identification continues throughout delivery.","The risk register records events, causes, owners, assessments, and responses. Qualitative analysis prioritizes; quantitative analysis models numerical effects.","EMV = probability × monetary outcome, summed over outcomes. Treat a loss as negative when using signed EMV.","Monte Carlo samples distributions repeatedly. Sensitivity analysis tests how changing inputs affects outcomes. Delphi gathers structured, often anonymous expert input.","Threat responses include avoid, mitigate, transfer, accept, and escalate. Opportunity responses include exploit, enhance, share, accept, and escalate/elevate.","Contingency plans respond to identified events; fallback plans apply when the initial response fails. Residual risks remain; secondary risks arise from a response."]},{"id":12,"title":"Procurement management","week":"Week 11 · Nov 18","pages":"505–538","pdfPages":"530–563","notes":["Make-or-buy decisions weigh internal provision versus procurement. Include capability and strategic fit as well as direct cost.","Fixed price generally places more defined-scope cost risk on the seller. Cost reimbursable places more on the buyer. Time and material fixes rates, not necessarily total cost.","The procurement SOW defines contracted work. RFP seeks proposals and approaches; RFQ seeks quotes for clearly specified requirements.","Conduct procurements obtains responses, selects sellers, and awards contracts. Predetermined evaluation criteria and bidder conferences support consistent selection.","Control procurements monitors performance and handles changes and closure. Informal directions can create constructive changes; use proper authority and procedures.","For a simple break-even comparison, equate total cost functions and solve. State assumptions about rates, time, and other costs."]},{"id":13,"title":"Stakeholder management","week":"Week 12 · Nov 25","pages":"539–560","pdfPages":"564–585","notes":["Identify internal and external stakeholders, including opponents. The register captures identification and assessment information.","Power-interest grid: high/high manage closely; high/low keep satisfied; low/high keep informed; low/low monitor. Reassess as circumstances change.","Engagement levels include unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive, and leading. Compare current and desired engagement to plan action.","Managing engagement means working with people on expectations and concerns. Monitoring engagement evaluates changing relationships and adjusts strategies.","Issue logs track current concerns, owners, and resolution. Some stakeholder assessments are sensitive and need appropriate handling.","Adaptive environments benefit from frequent feedback, transparent work, and timely stakeholder decisions. Do not postpone all input until final delivery."]}],"questions":[{"chapter":1,"topic":"Projects and operations","pages":"4–7","section":"Projects and operations","stem":"A college temporarily assigns a team to implement a new registration system. After launch, staff process registrations every term. Which classification is most appropriate?","options":["Registration processing becomes a project whenever a new term begins.","Both are projects because they use the same information system.","Both are operations because they support the same business function.","The implementation is a project; recurring registration processing is operations."],"correct":3,"explanation":"A project is a temporary effort that creates a unique product, service, or result. The implementation has a defined completion; routine processing continues as operations. Using technology does not determine the classification.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-001","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project attributes","pages":"6–7","section":"Project attributes","stem":"A team starts with a broad description of a proposed mobile service and adds detail as users clarify their needs. Which project attribute does this illustrate?","options":["Progressive elaboration","Resource leveling","Operational continuity","Portfolio balancing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Progressive elaboration means developing a project in steps and increasing detail as understanding improves. It is not permission to add uncontrolled scope; proposed changes still need appropriate management.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-002","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project constraints","pages":"7–9","section":"Project constraints","stem":"A sponsor adds two major features but refuses extra funding or a later delivery date. What should the project manager do first?","options":["Assess the effects on scope, time, cost, and other objectives, then discuss feasible trade-offs.","Accept the request because the sponsor is responsible for business benefits.","Treat the features as operations because the original scope was already approved.","Keep all baselines unchanged and require the team to absorb the work."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Scope, time, and cost interact, and quality, risk, and resources also matter. A requested scope increase requires impact analysis and a decision about trade-offs, not an unsupported promise that every objective can stay fixed.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-003","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Stakeholders and sponsors","pages":"9–11","section":"Stakeholders and sponsors","stem":"A system rollout affects users, a supplier, the sponsor, and an internal group opposing the change. Who belongs in stakeholder analysis?","options":["Only people who support the project","Only people employed by the organization","Only the sponsor and the users","All four groups"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Stakeholders include people involved in or affected by project activities. Suppliers and opponents can influence outcomes and must not be excluded merely because they are external or unsupportive.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-004","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project management knowledge areas","pages":"10–12","section":"Project management knowledge areas","stem":"A project manager coordinates a scope revision with schedule, budget, risk, and procurement decisions so that the project remains coherent. Which knowledge area is most directly involved?","options":["Project stakeholder management","Project resource management","Project integration management","Project communications management"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Integration coordinates the other knowledge areas throughout the project. Communications helps exchange information, but the decisive feature here is coordinating interdependent project decisions.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-005","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Tools and techniques","pages":"12–15","section":"Tools and techniques","stem":"Which pairing correctly matches a tool to its primary purpose?","options":["Gantt chart — calculate the discount rate that makes NPV zero","Stakeholder register — authorize the project manager to use organizational resources","Weighted scoring model — display actual versus planned task bars over time","Work breakdown structure — organize the project’s work into a hierarchy"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A WBS organizes the total scope hierarchically. A Gantt chart displays schedule information; a charter provides formal authorization; a weighted scoring model compares alternatives against weighted criteria.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-006","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project success","pages":"15–18","section":"Project success","stem":"A project meets its approved scope, deadline, and budget, but users reject the product and the expected business benefits do not occur. What is the best assessment?","options":["It failed only if the project manager lacks professional certification.","It is successful if every planned status meeting occurred.","It met important delivery targets, but overall success also requires considering customer satisfaction and results.","It must be considered fully successful because the triple constraint is the only criterion."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The chapter presents several ways to judge success: delivery objectives, customer or sponsor satisfaction, and achievement of the main goals or business results. Meeting constraints alone does not settle every measure of success.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-007","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Projects, programs, and portfolios","pages":"18–23","section":"Projects, programs, and portfolios","stem":"A company coordinates three related projects to obtain benefits unavailable if each were managed separately. It also selects investments across unrelated business units to meet strategic goals. Which terms fit these two activities, respectively?","options":["Portfolio management; program management","Program management; portfolio management","Project management; operations management","Operations management; project management"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Programs coordinate related projects for benefits and control that separate management would not provide. Portfolios organize and prioritize investments to serve strategy; their projects need not be directly related.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-008","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Organizational project management","pages":"21–23","section":"Organizational project management","stem":"An organization improves its project, program, and portfolio practices together so that strategy is translated into results. What does this most directly illustrate?","options":["A project’s product life cycle","Critical chain scheduling","Integrated change control for one project","Organizational project management"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Organizational project management aligns project, program, and portfolio management with organizational strategy. It is broader than improving the delivery processes of a single project.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-009","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project manager skills","pages":"24–29","section":"Project manager skills","stem":"A technically strong project manager struggles to connect project choices to the organization’s competitive goals. Which part of the ninth edition’s PMI talent triangle most directly needs development?","options":["Schedule network analysis","Procurement administration","Strategic and business management","Software programming"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The ninth edition’s talent triangle consists of technical project management, leadership, and strategic and business management. Understanding strategy and business value belongs to the third area; newer labels should not silently replace this edition’s terminology.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-010","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Leadership","pages":"27–29","section":"Leadership","stem":"A leader emphasizes service to others, team members’ growth, and the well-being of the community. Which leadership style best fits?","options":["Laissez-faire leadership","Charismatic leadership","Transactional leadership","Servant leadership"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Servant leadership puts service and others’ development first. Transactional leadership emphasizes exchanges such as rewards; laissez-faire is hands-off; charismatic leadership relies heavily on personal energy and influence.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-011","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Professional ethics","pages":"34–37","section":"Professional ethics","stem":"A project manager discovers that a status report omits a known delay and makes performance look better than it is. Which response best reflects professional ethics?","options":["Leave the report unchanged until someone outside the team detects the delay.","Report completion because the team expects to catch up next month.","Correct the report and communicate the delay honestly to the appropriate stakeholders.","Remove the delayed activity from the baseline without approval."],"correct":2,"explanation":"PMI’s ethical values include responsibility, respect, fairness, and honesty. Reporting an anticipated recovery as an accomplished result misrepresents current performance. Ethical reporting supports informed decisions.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-012","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Temporary projects","pages":"4–7","section":"Temporary projects","stem":"A project can be temporary even when its resulting product is intended to remain in use for many years.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Temporary describes the project effort, not necessarily the lifespan of its result. Building a long-lived system or facility can still be a project.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-013","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project sponsorship","pages":"6–7, 9–11","section":"Project sponsorship","stem":"A project sponsor is necessarily the same person as the project manager.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sponsor typically provides direction and funding support, while the project manager leads the work of meeting project objectives. These are distinct roles even if a particular organization sometimes combines responsibilities.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-014","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Professional development","pages":"30–35","section":"Professional development","stem":"Project management is a profession supported by organizations such as PMI and by credentials such as PMP.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chapter traces the development of the profession and describes PMI and its certifications. Membership totals, salary figures, and certification details in the book are historical snapshots, not current guarantees.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-015","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project management software","pages":"37–39","section":"Project management software","stem":"Using a sophisticated project management application removes the need for project management knowledge and judgment.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Software supports planning, analysis, and communication. People still have to define the right work, make sound assumptions, exercise judgment, and lead stakeholders; the tool cannot make those responsibilities disappear.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-016","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Systems approach and the three spheres","pages":"49–50","section":"Systems approach and the three spheres","stem":"A project team evaluates the business, organizational, and technological issues involved in changing an information system. Which term specifically describes this emphasis?","options":["Systems analysis","Scope decomposition","Critical path analysis","Systems management"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Systems management addresses business, technological, and organizational issues associated with creating, maintaining, or modifying systems. Systems analysis is the problem-solving approach that breaks a system into components and evaluates alternatives.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-017","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Three-sphere model","pages":"49–50","section":"Three-sphere model","stem":"A university’s new learning platform works technically and is affordable, but instructors resist changing their teaching practices. Which sphere most directly explains this obstacle?","options":["Technology","Business","Organization","Procurement"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Resistance, responsibilities, skills, and organizational change belong mainly to the organizational sphere. The technical solution and financial justification can be sound while organizational issues still threaten success.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-018","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Four organizational frames","pages":"51–53","section":"Four organizational frames","stem":"Two department heads compete for scarce specialists and use alliances to influence project priorities. Which organizational frame best explains the situation?","options":["Symbolic frame","Human resources frame","Structural frame","Political frame"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The political frame focuses on power, competing interests, coalitions, and resource conflicts. The structural frame concerns roles and coordination; the human resources frame concerns people’s needs; the symbolic frame concerns culture, meaning, and rituals.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-019","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Organizational structures","pages":"53–57","section":"Organizational structures","stem":"A project manager has moderate-to-high authority and controls the project budget, while functional departments still exist. Which structure is the best fit?","options":["Strong matrix","Functional","Weak matrix","Balanced matrix"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In a strong matrix, the project manager has more authority and generally controls the budget. A balanced matrix shares authority; a weak matrix and a functional organization give greater control to functional managers. Do not treat every matrix as strong.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-020","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Organizational culture","pages":"57–58","section":"Organizational culture","stem":"An organization rewards cooperative team outcomes and allows constructive disagreement about project decisions. Which two cultural dimensions are most directly illustrated?","options":["Group emphasis and conflict tolerance","Unit integration and control","Risk tolerance and open-systems focus","Member identity and means–ends orientation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Group emphasis concerns organizing work around groups rather than only individuals. Conflict tolerance concerns the extent to which employees are encouraged to air disagreements and criticism. These differ from tolerating risky decisions or coordinating across units.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-021","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Top management support","pages":"59–62","section":"Top management support","stem":"A project manager cannot obtain committed staff or timely cross-department decisions. Which action by senior management would most directly help?","options":["Require the project manager to perform every technical task personally.","Secure resources and cooperation and resolve decisions beyond the project manager’s authority.","Approve all future scope requests without impact analysis.","Replace stakeholder communication with a more detailed Gantt chart."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Top management support helps secure resources, obtain approvals, gain cooperation, and coach project managers. A plan or software tool cannot by itself supply the organizational authority needed to resolve these obstacles.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-022","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Project and product life cycles","pages":"63–66","section":"Project and product life cycles","stem":"A company develops a product, later performs a major upgrade, and eventually retires it. How can project and product life cycles relate?","options":["Every product must use the same project life cycle and development approach.","The product life cycle must end when its first development project closes.","Several projects may occur within the product’s longer life cycle.","A product life cycle is simply another name for the five project management process groups."],"correct":2,"explanation":"A product may continue through development, operation, improvement, and retirement, with multiple projects along the way. A project life cycle describes the phases of one project; process groups are a different classification.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-023","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Development approaches","pages":"64–66","section":"Development approaches","stem":"A team repeatedly refines a prototype to improve its understanding of a solution. Another team delivers additional usable features in successive releases. Which distinction is most accurate?","options":["Neither can be used in a hybrid approach.","The first emphasizes iteration; the second emphasizes increments.","The first is always predictive; the second is always waterfall.","Iteration refers to adding features; increments refer only to correcting defects."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Iteration emphasizes refining through repeated cycles. Incremental development emphasizes adding functionality through successive deliveries. These can be combined, so one label does not automatically exclude the other.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-024","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Phase reviews","pages":"63–67","section":"Phase reviews","stem":"Before allowing a costly next phase, executives review performance and whether the project remains worth doing. What is this checkpoint?","options":["A daily Scrum","A phase exit, stage gate, or kill point","A scope baseline","A work package"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Phase reviews assess progress and continued justification before proceeding. They can lead to continuation, changes, or termination; completing a phase does not make continuation automatic.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-025","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Distinctive features of IT projects","pages":"67–69","section":"Distinctive features of IT projects","stem":"A manager assumes two IT projects will need identical skills and coordination because both involve computers. Which textbook point most directly challenges this assumption?","options":["IT project managers can ignore organizational goals when technology is complex.","IT projects vary greatly in scope, technology, participants, and business context.","Every IT team consists only of programmers.","IT projects are always smaller than construction projects."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The chapter emphasizes the diversity of IT projects, team members’ backgrounds, and rapidly changing technologies. A shared IT label does not establish identical requirements or management needs.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-026","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Outsourcing and globalization","pages":"69–72","section":"Outsourcing and globalization","stem":"A Canadian organization hires an independent Canadian company to provide testing services. Which statement is correct?","options":["This is outsourcing, even though the supplier is in the same country.","It is a virtual team arrangement only, so it cannot be outsourcing.","It is outsourcing only if the supplier is located overseas.","It is insourcing because both companies operate in Canada."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Outsourcing obtains goods or services from an outside source. Offshoring concerns location in another country. Outsourcing and offshoring can occur together, but they are not synonyms.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-027","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Agile values","pages":"73–75","section":"Agile values","stem":"A customer discovers a valuable requirement after initial planning. Which response best reflects the Agile Manifesto’s values?","options":["Reject the request because agile teams never revise a plan.","Collaborate with the customer and adapt plans to the new information.","Replace working software demonstrations with comprehensive reports.","Eliminate all documentation and contractual commitments."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Agile values customer collaboration and responding to change, while also valuing working software and individuals’ interactions. The manifesto values the items on the right as well; it does not say plans, documentation, processes, or contracts have no value.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-028","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Systems philosophy","pages":"49–50","section":"Systems philosophy","stem":"Systems thinking encourages a project manager to consider how a project fits and affects the larger organization.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A systems philosophy takes a holistic view of interacting components and their environment. Optimizing a technical component in isolation can harm the larger business system.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-029","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Life-cycle uncertainty and influence","pages":"63–64","section":"Life-cycle uncertainty and influence","stem":"In a typical project life cycle, uncertainty is usually lowest at the beginning and increases steadily toward completion.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Uncertainty and risk are generally greatest early, when much is unknown. Stakeholders also typically have greater ability to influence outcomes early; changes tend to become more costly later.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-030","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Virtual teams","pages":"72–73","section":"Virtual teams","stem":"Virtual teams can reduce location constraints, but effective communication, trust, and team leadership still require deliberate attention.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Virtual arrangements offer benefits such as broader access to expertise and flexible location. Distance and reduced face-to-face contact can create coordination and relationship challenges; technology alone does not remove them.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-031","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Adaptive development","pages":"73–77","section":"Adaptive development","stem":"An agile approach eliminates the need for planning because requirements may change.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Agile teams plan and replan, often at release and iteration levels. Adaptation changes the timing and detail of planning, not the need to plan or coordinate work.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-032","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Five process groups","pages":"86–90","section":"Five process groups","stem":"A team compares actual progress with the plan and determines whether corrective action is needed. Which process group best fits this activity?","options":["Initiating","Monitoring and controlling","Closing","Executing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Monitoring and controlling measures progress against objectives, identifies variances, and supports corrective action. Executing performs the planned work; observing and evaluating its performance is a different function.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-033","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Process groups and phases","pages":"86–90","section":"Process groups and phases","stem":"A predictive project has design, development, and deployment phases. Which statement about the five process groups is most accurate?","options":["Each phase must correspond to exactly one process group.","Initiating can occur only once in an organization’s product life cycle.","Relevant process groups can recur within phases and interact throughout the project.","Monitoring and controlling begins only after executing is complete."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The process groups are not life-cycle phases. Planning, executing, and monitoring and controlling interact, and processes can recur within multiple phases as needed.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-034","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Process-to-knowledge-area mapping","pages":"91–92","section":"Process-to-knowledge-area mapping","stem":"Which pair belongs to the initiating process group in the ninth edition’s process mapping?","options":["Develop project charter; identify stakeholders","Direct and manage project work; manage project knowledge","Validate scope; close project or phase","Develop project management plan; collect requirements"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The initiating group includes Develop Project Charter in integration management and Identify Stakeholders in stakeholder management. Developing the management plan is planning; validation is monitoring and controlling.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-035","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Process-to-knowledge-area mapping","pages":"91–92","section":"Process-to-knowledge-area mapping","stem":"Which set contains only monitoring and controlling processes?","options":["Identify stakeholders, collect requirements, and close project or phase","Define scope, develop schedule, and plan risk responses","Validate scope, control schedule, and monitor risks","Manage quality, acquire resources, and conduct procurements"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Validate, control, and monitor processes in this set check performance or results. Defining scope and developing the schedule are planning; managing quality and conducting procurements are executing.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-036","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Methodologies and tailoring","pages":"93–95","section":"Methodologies and tailoring","stem":"A company uses PMBOK concepts but adapts its templates, approval steps, and delivery approach to project size and risk. What is the best interpretation?","options":["It is abandoning project management because every project must use identical procedures.","It is tailoring a methodology that explains how the organization will manage its projects.","It is using the PMBOK Guide as a complete, mandatory step-by-step methodology.","It is converting all management processes into product-development phases."],"correct":1,"explanation":"A methodology describes how work should be done; a body of knowledge describes recognized concepts and processes. Tailoring selects and adapts practices to context rather than mechanically applying everything.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-037","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"JWD Consulting: business case and charter","pages":"95–108","section":"JWD Consulting: business case and charter","stem":"In the predictive JWD Consulting case, management first considers why the intranet project is worthwhile and then formally authorizes it. Which documents serve these purposes, respectively?","options":["Project charter; business case","Stakeholder register; issue log","WBS dictionary; scope statement","Business case; project charter"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The business case supports the investment decision with needs, benefits, costs, and justification. The charter formally authorizes the project. The original JWD case uses these documents before detailed planning.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-038","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Stakeholder documents","pages":"105–108","section":"Stakeholder documents","stem":"A manager wants one document listing stakeholder identities and contacts and a separate restricted document with candid assessments of influence and engagement strategies. Which pair fits the textbook case?","options":["Issue log; product backlog","Project charter; schedule baseline","Business case; scope statement","Stakeholder register; stakeholder management strategy"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The register records stakeholder information. The strategy can include sensitive assessments and should be handled appropriately. The distinction prevents assuming that every stakeholder analysis should be distributed openly.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-039","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Team charter","pages":"109–110","section":"Team charter","stem":"A team records how it will handle meetings, disagreements, decisions, and communication. What document is it creating?","options":["Team charter","Scope baseline","Business case","Project charter"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A team charter establishes team values, agreements, and working practices. A project charter authorizes the project and the project manager; it is not interchangeable with team operating agreements.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-040","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"JWD Consulting: predictive planning","pages":"110–116","section":"JWD Consulting: predictive planning","stem":"JWD’s sample work breakdown structure includes initiating, planning, executing, monitoring and controlling, and closing work. What lesson should a project manager take from this?","options":["Project management work must be included in the project’s scope and estimates.","A WBS must always use the same process-group headings as the JWD example.","Management effort can be excluded because it creates no product feature.","Only customer-facing software features belong in a WBS."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The case makes project management work visible instead of treating it as free overhead. Its particular organizing structure is an example, not a universal rule that every WBS must follow.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-041","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"JWD Consulting: agile and predictive approaches","pages":"129–138","section":"JWD Consulting: agile and predictive approaches","stem":"Which comparison best reflects the two JWD Consulting case studies?","options":["The predictive version delivers no usable result at project completion.","The agile version makes financial objectives irrelevant.","The agile version uses multiple releases and sprints while retaining business justification and project coordination.","The agile version removes the sponsor and all planning documents."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The agile case pursues the same broad business purpose through iterative delivery, with three releases and four-week sprints. It still uses justification, planning, stakeholder involvement, and management processes; agile does not make these disappear.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-042","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Scrum roles","pages":"130–134","section":"Scrum roles","stem":"Users disagree about which feature should be built next, and the development team also faces an organizational impediment. Which pairing best describes the Scrum roles?","options":["ScrumMaster prioritizes business value; product owner assigns every developer’s daily work.","Development team authorizes organizational funding; sponsor runs each daily Scrum.","Product owner prioritizes the backlog; ScrumMaster helps remove impediments.","Product owner alone estimates every task; ScrumMaster accepts all deliverables without user input."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The product owner is responsible for maximizing value and ordering the product backlog. The ScrumMaster facilitates Scrum and helps address impediments; the team organizes how to do its work. These responsibilities are not a command-and-control task-assignment hierarchy.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-043","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Scrum events","pages":"131–138","section":"Scrum events","stem":"At the end of a sprint, stakeholders inspect the completed product work. Later, the team discusses how to improve collaboration in the next sprint. Which events are these, respectively?","options":["Sprint review; sprint retrospective","Sprint retrospective; daily Scrum","Sprint planning; sprint review","Daily Scrum; sprint planning"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The review inspects product results with stakeholders and informs future work. The retrospective examines how the team worked and how to improve. A daily Scrum is a short coordination event, not either end-of-sprint event.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-044","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Process group effort","pages":"86–90","section":"Process group effort","stem":"Executing typically uses the greatest share of project resources and time.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chapter emphasizes that executing normally consumes the most resources because it carries out the work. That is different from saying it contains the greatest number of named processes; the mapping contains many planning processes.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-045","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Edition-specific process mapping","pages":"91–92","section":"Edition-specific process mapping","stem":"In the ninth edition’s mapping, Estimate Activity Resources is a project schedule management process.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The ninth edition maps Estimate Activity Resources to project resource management in the planning group. Earlier editions placed it in time management, which can explain conflicting older notes.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-046","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Closing","pages":"126–129","section":"Closing","stem":"A project that is terminated early still requires appropriate closing activities.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Closure is needed even for terminated projects: document the outcome and reasons, finalize appropriate arrangements, preserve lessons, and handle remaining work or transition. Cancellation does not justify simply abandoning records.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-047","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Scrum artifacts","pages":"131–132","section":"Scrum artifacts","stem":"The sprint backlog and product backlog must contain exactly the same work at all times.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The product backlog represents the broader ordered work for the product. The sprint backlog focuses on work selected for a particular sprint and the associated delivery plan; it is not a duplicate of the entire product backlog.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-048","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Burndown charts","pages":"131–138","section":"Burndown charts","stem":"A burndown chart helps track how much work remains over time.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A burndown chart displays remaining work, supporting discussion of progress and forecasts. It is not simply a graph of money spent or proof that all remaining work will finish on time.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-049","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Agile estimating","pages":"133–138","section":"Agile estimating","stem":"Because the agile JWD example associates a story point with a number of hours, every Scrum team must use that same conversion.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The JWD case uses its own estimating convention. That convention is not a universal Scrum rule; teams must understand their own estimates and avoid treating one case’s point-to-hour relationship as a standard.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-050","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Integration processes","pages":"152–153","section":"Integration processes","stem":"Which sequence correctly names the integration management processes from the ninth edition’s seven-process list?","options":["Develop business case; approve funding; define activities; assign staff; test product; invoice customer; archive files","Develop charter; develop management plan; direct and manage work; manage knowledge; monitor and control work; perform integrated change control; close project or phase","Identify stakeholders; plan communications; manage communications; monitor communications; acquire resources; control resources; close project or phase","Develop charter; collect requirements; create WBS; develop schedule; control costs; validate scope; close project or phase"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Figure 4-1 and the enumerated list contain seven integration processes, including Manage Project Knowledge. Some nearby prose says six; that is an internal inconsistency, not a reason to omit knowledge management. The other lists mix different areas or informal activities.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-051","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Strategic planning and selection","pages":"156–161","section":"Strategic planning and selection","stem":"During SWOT analysis, a company identifies strong in-house analytics expertise and a growing external market for analytics services. How should these be classified?","options":["Weakness; opportunity","Strength; opportunity","Strength; threat","Opportunity; strength"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats concern the external environment. Project selection should connect these findings to organizational needs, available funding, and commitment.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-052","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Net present value","pages":"162–165","section":"Net present value","stem":"A project costs $10,000 now and produces one cash inflow of $12,100 at the end of year 2. At a 10% annual discount rate, what is its NPV?","options":["$0","$1,000","$2,100","−$1,000"],"correct":0,"explanation":"NPV = −10,000 + 12,100/(1.10)^2 = −10,000 + 10,000 = $0. Time-zero cash is not discounted, and the year-2 inflow is discounted for two years. The undiscounted surplus of $2,100 is not NPV.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-053","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Return on investment","pages":"165–166","section":"Return on investment","stem":"A proposal has discounted benefits of $150,000 and discounted costs of $100,000. Using the textbook formula, what is ROI?","options":["50%","150%","66.7%","33.3%"],"correct":0,"explanation":"ROI = (benefits − costs)/costs = (150,000 − 100,000)/100,000 = 0.50 = 50%. Dividing benefits directly by costs gives 150%, but that is a benefit-to-cost comparison, not this ROI formula.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-054","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Payback analysis","pages":"166–167","section":"Payback analysis","stem":"A project requires $60,000 at time zero and returns $20,000 at the end of each year for four years. Ignoring discounting, when does cumulative net cash flow first reach zero?","options":["End of year 4","At the start of year 1","End of year 2","End of year 3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Cumulative net cash flow is −60,000 initially, then −40,000, −20,000, and $0 at the ends of years 1, 2, and 3. This question specifies year-end flows and undiscounted payback; neither interpolation nor discounting is needed.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-055","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Weighted scoring models","pages":"167–169","section":"Weighted scoring models","stem":"A selection model weights strategic fit at 50%, financial return at 30%, and feasibility at 20%. Project A scores 80, 60, and 90; Project B scores 70, 90, and 70. Higher is better. What is the result?","options":["Both score 76; this model alone does not break the tie.","A scores 78 and B scores 74, so A wins.","A scores 230 and B scores 230, so weights have no purpose.","B wins because financial return must always override the other criteria."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A = 0.50(80) + 0.30(60) + 0.20(90) = 76. B = 0.50(70) + 0.30(90) + 0.20(70) = 76. The weighted total is tied; additional justified criteria or judgment are needed. Unweighted sums do not implement the stated model.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-056","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Project charter","pages":"169–173","section":"Project charter","stem":"An organization needs to formally authorize a project and give its project manager authority to apply organizational resources. Which document serves this purpose?","options":["Detailed activity list","Project charter","Team charter","Stakeholder register"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A project charter formally authorizes the project and the project manager’s authority. A team charter concerns working agreements; a stakeholder register and activity list support management but do not provide equivalent authorization.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-057","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Project management plan","pages":"174–176","section":"Project management plan","stem":"A manager says the project management plan is complete because a Gantt chart has been prepared. Which response is best?","options":["The plan integrates subsidiary plans and baselines and explains how the project will be executed, monitored, controlled, and closed.","The plan should contain only the project sponsor’s signature.","The plan is simply another name for the business case.","The Gantt chart is sufficient because all other knowledge areas are optional."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A schedule is only part of the overall project management plan. Integration connects scope, schedule, cost, and the other management approaches; a schedule graphic alone cannot explain how all these aspects will be managed.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-058","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Managing project knowledge","pages":"180–182","section":"Managing project knowledge","stem":"An experienced analyst mentors a colleague about how to recognize subtle signs of stakeholder resistance that are difficult to write down. What kind of knowledge is primarily being shared?","options":["Tacit knowledge","Explicit knowledge","A formal change request","A schedule baseline"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Tacit knowledge involves experience, insights, and know-how that can be hard to articulate. Explicit knowledge is easier to document in words, diagrams, or numbers. Knowledge management needs interpersonal sharing as well as written repositories.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-059","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Monitoring and controlling work","pages":"182–183","section":"Monitoring and controlling work","stem":"Actual progress is behind the approved plan. The manager proposes an action designed to bring future performance back into alignment with that plan. What is the action called?","options":["Project authorization","Defect repair","Corrective action","Preventive action"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Corrective action realigns performance with the plan. Preventive action reduces the probability of future negative consequences; defect repair corrects a nonconforming product or component. The response addresses an existing performance deviation.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-060","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Integrated change control","pages":"183–187","section":"Integrated change control","stem":"A user requests a feature that will add two weeks and increase cost. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":["Start the work immediately because the requester is a stakeholder.","Document the request, evaluate its integrated impacts, and obtain a decision through the agreed change-control process.","Update the baseline first so the change cannot create a variance.","Reject the request automatically because any change proves planning failed."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Integrated change control evaluates effects across the project and applies the agreed approval authority. Useful changes can be approved, but informal acceptance or silently changing baselines undermines control. Not every small change necessarily needs the same approval level.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-061","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Configuration management","pages":"185–187","section":"Configuration management","stem":"Several teams are using different versions of an approved specification. Which practice most directly addresses identifying the authorized version and controlling its changes?","options":["Payback analysis","Portfolio balancing","Team motivation","Configuration management"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Configuration management identifies and documents product characteristics, controls changes to them, and supports verification and reporting of configuration status. It complements change control by keeping the correct, consistent versions in use.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-062","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Investment appraisal","pages":"162–166","section":"Investment appraisal","stem":"For an independent proposal evaluated at the required discount rate, a positive NPV indicates that discounted benefits exceed discounted costs.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"That is the meaning of a positive NPV under the model’s assumptions. It supports financial attractiveness, but does not remove risk, funding limits, or other selection criteria.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-063","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Internal rate of return","pages":"165–166","section":"Internal rate of return","stem":"The internal rate of return is the discount rate that makes a project’s NPV equal to zero.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"IRR is defined by NPV = 0. Do not confuse it with ROI, which compares net benefits with costs, or payback, which measures how long recovery takes.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-064","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Balanced scorecard","pages":"169","section":"Balanced scorecard","stem":"A balanced scorecard evaluates an organization using financial performance alone.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A balanced scorecard translates strategy into multiple measures, including customer, internal business process, and learning and growth perspectives as well as financial measures. Its point is a broader view of performance.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-065","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Knowledge management","pages":"180–182","section":"Knowledge management","stem":"Teams should capture and share useful project knowledge during the project rather than waiting until closure.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Manage Project Knowledge operates during the work. Sharing lessons promptly can improve current decisions, while closure also preserves knowledge for future use.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-066","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Baseline control","pages":"183–187","section":"Baseline control","stem":"When actual performance is worse than planned, the manager should automatically revise the baseline to match actual results.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A baseline is an approved reference for measuring performance. Replacing it automatically erases meaningful variances. Baseline changes require the applicable change-control process; forecasts and actual results can be updated without pretending the original baseline was met.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-067","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Project closure","pages":"188–190","section":"Project closure","stem":"Completing the technical deliverables makes formal transition, final reporting, and project-record updates unnecessary.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Closing includes appropriate administrative work, transition of the result, and updates to records and organizational knowledge. Technical completion alone does not ensure responsibilities have been transferred or the project has been formally closed.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-068","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Scope management processes","pages":"202–204","section":"Scope management processes","stem":"Which sequence lists the six project scope management processes in the ninth edition?","options":["Plan scope management; collect requirements; define scope; create WBS; validate scope; control scope","Collect requirements; develop charter; acquire resources; manage quality; close project; validate scope","Plan requirements; estimate costs; define scope; validate schedule; control risks; close scope","Develop charter; define activities; sequence activities; estimate durations; develop schedule; control schedule"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The first four listed scope processes belong to planning. Validate Scope and Control Scope belong to monitoring and controlling. Activity definition and sequencing are schedule processes, not substitutes for scope processes.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-069","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Scope and requirements management plans","pages":"203–205","section":"Scope and requirements management plans","stem":"A manager wants to specify how requirements will be analyzed, documented, prioritized, and tracked. Which document most directly addresses this?","options":["Stakeholder register","Schedule baseline","Requirements management plan","Project charter"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The requirements management plan describes how requirements will be handled. The scope management plan addresses how scope will be defined, developed, monitored, controlled, and validated. Their purposes are related but not identical.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-070","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Collecting requirements","pages":"205–209","section":"Collecting requirements","stem":"Users struggle to describe what they need from an unfamiliar interface. Which technique most directly lets them react to an early model before the full system is built?","options":["Earned value analysis","Backward-pass calculation","Prototyping","Crashing"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A prototype gives users something tangible to evaluate, helping uncover and clarify requirements. The other techniques address schedule compression, performance measurement, or network calculations rather than eliciting interface requirements.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-071","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Requirements traceability matrix","pages":"208–209","section":"Requirements traceability matrix","stem":"A tester wants to connect each requirement to its source, implementation status, and verification evidence so that no approved requirement is lost. Which tool is most appropriate?","options":["Risk probability-impact matrix","Organizational breakdown structure","Requirements traceability matrix","Gantt chart"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A traceability matrix links requirements with relevant attributes across the project. It supports tracking coverage and changes; simply listing tasks or risks does not establish the requirement-to-evidence links.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-072","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Project scope statement","pages":"209–212","section":"Project scope statement","stem":"Two stakeholders disagree about whether migration of historical records is included in a system project. Which document should most directly clarify approved deliverables, boundaries, and exclusions?","options":["Procurement invoice","Project scope statement","Daily team attendance record","Project organization chart"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The scope statement describes product scope, deliverables, acceptance criteria, and boundaries or exclusions. Clear exclusions reduce disputes about work that stakeholders might otherwise assume is included.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-073","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Creating the WBS","pages":"213–219","section":"Creating the WBS","stem":"A team breaks a large deliverable into smaller components until the lowest-level components can be estimated, assigned, and managed. What are those lowest-level WBS components called?","options":["Milestones","Portfolio categories","Work packages","Process groups"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A work package is the lowest level of the WBS. It organizes a manageable piece of scope and is later decomposed into schedule activities as needed. A milestone is a zero-duration significant event, not a package of work.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-074","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"WBS scope coverage","pages":"213–224","section":"WBS scope coverage","stem":"Which proposal best follows the textbook’s WBS principles?","options":["Include only visible product features and leave management work outside the scope.","Use the WBS to approve unrequested features that developers consider useful.","Include the full project scope, including project management work, without counting the same work in multiple places.","Repeat shared work under every department so no team forgets it."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The WBS accounts for the total work needed for the project. Overlaps can double-count effort and costs, while omissions leave work unplanned. Valuable-sounding extra features still require appropriate scope decisions.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-075","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Approaches to developing a WBS","pages":"219–222","section":"Approaches to developing a WBS","stem":"A manager starts with the WBS of a similar completed project and adapts it to the current project. Which approach is this?","options":["Mind-mapping approach","Analogy approach","Bottom-up approach","Parametric duration estimating"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The analogy approach uses a similar project as a starting point. Bottom-up starts with detailed work and groups it; mind mapping develops branches from a central idea. Reusing an example still requires checking differences in the new project.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-076","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"WBS dictionary","pages":"222–224","section":"WBS dictionary","stem":"The label “Data conversion” in a WBS is too vague for the assigned team. Where should detailed work-package descriptions and related information be recorded?","options":["WBS dictionary","Product vision slogan","Project portfolio ranking","Sprint burndown chart"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The WBS dictionary provides detailed information about WBS components, such as work descriptions and other relevant details. Short WBS labels alone may not communicate the full meaning of a work package.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-077","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Scope baseline","pages":"223–224","section":"Scope baseline","stem":"Which set makes up the scope baseline?","options":["Business case, benefits management plan, and procurement contract","Activity list, schedule network diagram, and resource calendar","Project charter, stakeholder register, and issue log","Approved project scope statement, WBS, and WBS dictionary"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The scope baseline consists of the approved scope statement, WBS, and WBS dictionary. These provide a reference for scope control; other documents support the project but are not this baseline’s three components.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-078","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Validate scope and control quality","pages":"224–227","section":"Validate scope and control quality","stem":"Testing has confirmed that a deliverable meets specified quality requirements. The customer now inspects it to provide formal acceptance. Which process is this?","options":["Control quality","Validate scope","Define scope","Manage project knowledge"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Validate Scope focuses on formal acceptance of completed deliverables. Control Quality focuses on correctness and meeting quality requirements, producing verified deliverables that can be presented for acceptance. Verification and acceptance are related but distinct.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-079","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Scope control","pages":"227–231","section":"Scope control","stem":"A client casually asks a developer to add a useful report that is outside approved scope. What is the best response?","options":["Treat every client suggestion as automatically accepted scope.","Add it without documentation because it improves customer satisfaction.","Remove an approved feature without consulting affected stakeholders to make room.","Record the request and assess its impacts through the project’s change-control process."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Uncontrolled additions cause scope creep. The request may be beneficial, but its cost, schedule, risk, and scope impacts need evaluation and the appropriate decision. Agile projects also manage priorities and commitments rather than accepting all requests without control.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-080","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Requirements and scope","pages":"205–212","section":"Requirements and scope","stem":"Requirements describe stakeholder needs or conditions to be met; defining scope develops a detailed description of the project and product.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Requirements collection and scope definition are linked, but they are not identical. Requirements inform the detailed boundaries, deliverables, and acceptance criteria in the scope statement.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-081","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Work package responsibility","pages":"222–224","section":"Work package responsibility","stem":"Assigning one person accountability for a work package means that only that person may perform its work.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Clear accountability does not prevent multiple people from contributing. The textbook’s WBS guidance distinguishes responsibility for a component from the number of people doing the work.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-082","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Scope and activity sequencing","pages":"213–224","section":"Scope and activity sequencing","stem":"A WBS alone establishes all activity dependencies and the order in which every task must occur.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A WBS organizes scope hierarchically. Schedule management defines activities and sequences their dependencies; the hierarchy is not itself a complete schedule network.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-083","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"User involvement and scope","pages":"228–231","section":"User involvement and scope","stem":"Continuing user involvement and clear requirements can reduce scope problems, but they do not remove the need to control changes.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chapter stresses user input, realistic expectations, and clear requirements. These improve understanding, while scope control still addresses proposed changes and protects agreed commitments.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-084","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Schedule management processes","pages":"243–247","section":"Schedule management processes","stem":"A team takes WBS work packages and identifies the specific tasks needed to produce their deliverables. Which schedule process is it performing?","options":["Estimate activity durations","Create WBS","Sequence activities","Define activities"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Define Activities identifies the specific actions needed to produce deliverables. Creating the WBS decomposes scope; sequencing identifies activity relationships; estimating durations determines how long activities will take.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-085","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Dependencies","pages":"248–251","section":"Dependencies","stem":"A team chooses to code the reporting module after the search module because this has worked well before, although neither physically requires the other. What kind of dependency is this?","options":["An unavoidable finish-to-finish relationship","Mandatory dependency","External dependency imposed by law","Discretionary dependency"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A discretionary dependency reflects preferred logic, experience, or a chosen approach. Mandatory dependencies arise from the nature of the work or binding requirements. External/internal and mandatory/discretionary are different dimensions, not interchangeable labels.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-086","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Precedence relationships","pages":"250–252","section":"Precedence relationships","stem":"Activity B cannot start until Activity A starts, but it need not wait for A to finish. Which relationship is specified?","options":["Start-to-start","Start-to-finish","Finish-to-start","Finish-to-finish"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A start-to-start relationship links the successor’s start to the predecessor’s start. It does not require simultaneous starts unless additional constraints do so. The chapter’s figure illustrates this; nearby prose reverses the predecessor/successor direction. Follow the relationship shown in the figure.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-087","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Effort and duration","pages":"253–254","section":"Effort and duration","stem":"An activity requires 24 person-hours of effort. One person can devote 6 productive hours per workday to it. With no interruptions or other constraints, what is its duration?","options":["6 workdays","4 workdays","3 workdays","24 workdays"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Duration = effort / productive availability = 24/6 = 4 workdays. Effort measures labor input; duration measures elapsed working time. Assuming an 8-hour contribution would contradict the availability stated here.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-088","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Milestones and Gantt charts","pages":"255–259","section":"Milestones and Gantt charts","stem":"A schedule marks “Sponsor accepts final design” as a significant event with no duration. What is it?","options":["A milestone","A feeding buffer","A discretionary dependency","A work package containing all design tasks"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A milestone represents a significant event and has zero duration. Work leading to it has duration and should be represented by activities; the milestone itself is not the whole design effort.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-089","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Critical path method","pages":"259–262","section":"Critical path method","stem":"A project has two independent start-to-finish paths: A(3 days) → B(5 days) → D(2 days), and C(6 days) → E(3 days). With no resource constraints or lags, what is the earliest project completion and critical path?","options":["9 days; C–E","8 days; A–B","10 days; A–B–D","19 days; both paths combined"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The paths take 3+5+2 = 10 days and 6+3 = 9 days. The longest path sets the minimum project duration under the stated assumptions. Parallel path durations are not added to get total elapsed time.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-090","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Forward pass at a merge","pages":"259–264","section":"Forward pass at a merge","stem":"Activity D has two finish-to-start predecessors. A finishes at time 7 and B at time 11. With zero lag, when can D start at the earliest?","options":["Time 7","Time 11","Time 18","Time 4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"D must wait for both predecessors, so its early start is the maximum predecessor early finish: max(7,11) = 11. Taking the smaller value would start before B is ready.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-091","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Total float and free float","pages":"261–264","section":"Total float and free float","stem":"Activity A starts at time 0 and lasts 2 days; B follows A for 2 days. Independent activity C lasts 6 days. The project finishes only when B and C are done. With zero-lag finish-to-start logic, what are A’s total float and free float?","options":["Total float 0 days; free float 2 days","Total float 2 days; free float 2 days","Total float 4 days; free float 0 days","Total float 2 days; free float 0 days"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The A–B path takes 4 days while C takes 6, giving A 2 days of total float before project completion is affected. But B’s early start is immediately after A at time 2, so any delay to A delays B’s early start: A has zero free float. Float along a path is shared, not separately spendable by every activity.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-092","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Critical paths and crashing","pages":"262–265","section":"Critical paths and crashing","stem":"A project has two critical paths of 12 days. A proposed one-day reduction affects an activity on only one path. What happens to the earliest project finish, assuming the other path is unchanged?","options":["It becomes 11 days.","It becomes 10 days.","It cannot be determined without the project’s total cost.","It remains 12 days."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The unchanged critical path still takes 12 days and controls completion. To shorten a project with multiple critical paths, reductions must address every controlling path, perhaps by shortening a shared critical activity.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-093","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Schedule compression","pages":"263–265","section":"Schedule compression","stem":"A manager overlaps design and construction activities that were originally planned sequentially. Which technique is this, and what is a typical trade-off?","options":["Resource leveling; guaranteed shorter duration","Crashing; guaranteed cost savings","Scope validation; fewer acceptance requirements","Fast tracking; greater risk of rework and coordination problems"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Fast tracking overlaps work that would otherwise be sequential, which can shorten the schedule but create risk and rework. Crashing seeks the most schedule reduction for the least additional cost, often by adding resources to critical activities.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-094","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Critical chain scheduling","pages":"265–268","section":"Critical chain scheduling","stem":"A schedule explicitly considers scarce resources, minimizes harmful multitasking, and places buffers at the project’s end and where noncritical work feeds critical-chain work. Which approach is this?","options":["Scope decomposition","Pure critical path analysis without resource considerations","Critical chain scheduling","Weighted scoring"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Critical chain scheduling incorporates resource constraints and uses a project buffer and feeding buffers to protect completion. It differs from simply adding arbitrary padding to every task or assuming resources are unlimited.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-095","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"PERT weighted estimates","pages":"268–269","section":"PERT weighted estimates","stem":"An activity’s optimistic duration is 2 days, most likely duration is 5 days, and pessimistic duration is 14 days. Using the textbook’s PERT weighted average, what is the estimate?","options":["7 days","6 days","5 days","9 days"],"correct":1,"explanation":"PERT estimate = (optimistic + 4 × most likely + pessimistic)/6 = (2 + 4×5 + 14)/6 = 36/6 = 6 days. The simple unweighted average is 7, which is not this formula.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-096","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Network diagrams","pages":"249–252","section":"Network diagrams","stem":"In activity-on-arrow diagrams, arrows represent activities; in precedence diagramming, nodes represent activities.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The two diagramming conventions use different symbols for work. The chapter notes that precedence diagramming can represent several relationship types, while traditional activity-on-arrow focuses on finish-to-start logic.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-097","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Critical activities","pages":"259–265","section":"Critical activities","stem":"The activity with the longest individual duration must lie on the project’s critical path.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Criticality depends on the total length of connected paths and network logic, not just one activity’s duration. A long task on a shorter parallel path can have float.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-098","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Schedule control","pages":"270–275","section":"Schedule control","stem":"A project manager should verify reported progress and assess schedule changes rather than relying only on a schedule file that looks current.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Schedule control depends on reliable performance information and appropriate responses to change. Updating a chart without verifying actual work can conceal slippage; the chapter’s closing case illustrates the risk of misleading progress reports.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-099","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Agile scheduling","pages":"269–275","section":"Agile scheduling","stem":"Using a fixed sprint duration means that the team must accept every newly requested feature into that sprint without revisiting priorities or commitments.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Timeboxing fixes the iteration’s time boundary; it does not create unlimited capacity. Agile teams prioritize and plan work within capacity and manage new information. Adaptation is not uncontrolled expansion of a sprint’s work.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-100","origin":"Original practice scenario or question; textbook concepts, not an actual exam item."},{"id":"M091","chapter":7,"topic":"Sunk costs","section":"Basic Principles of Cost Management","stem":"A cancelled platform has already consumed $300,000 that cannot be recovered. Continuing would cost more than the expected future benefit. Which treatment of the past spending best supports the decision?","options":["Treat the past spending as a new benefit.","Count sunk costs as future revenue.","Continue solely to recover the historical spending.","Exclude sunk costs from the forward-looking comparison."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Sunk costs have already been incurred and cannot be changed by the decision. Compare relevant future costs and benefits rather than continuing to justify past spending.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M092","chapter":7,"topic":"Life cycle costing","section":"Basic Principles of Cost Management","stem":"System A is cheaper to buy but expensive to operate. System B costs more initially but uses fewer support resources. Which evaluation best avoids choosing solely on purchase price?","options":["Ignore maintenance costs because delivery ends at launch.","Compare total ownership costs across the systems' life cycles.","Select whichever has the lowest acquisition price.","Treat all recurring costs as sunk costs before purchase."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Life cycle costing considers acquisition and later operating and support costs. Costs not yet incurred are not sunk merely because they recur.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M093","chapter":7,"topic":"Analogous estimates","section":"Tools and Techniques for Cost Estimation","stem":"A manager has little detail about a proposed application and estimates its cost using a similar completed project, adjusted for known differences. Which estimating technique is being applied?","options":["Earned value measurement","Bottom-up estimating","Analogous estimating","Reserve analysis only"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Analogous estimates use comparable prior work. Bottom-up estimating sums detailed components, which are not yet available in this scenario.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M094","chapter":7,"topic":"Bottom-up estimates","section":"Tools and Techniques for Cost Estimation","stem":"Team leads estimate each detailed work component and the manager aggregates those estimates into a project total. Which cost-estimating approach most directly describes this procedure?","options":["Simple payback analysis","Bottom-up estimating","Analogous estimating","Top-down allocation without detail"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Bottom-up estimates build a total from individual components. They can be detailed but take effort and depend on the quality of underlying work estimates.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M095","chapter":7,"topic":"Parametric estimates","section":"Tools and Techniques for Cost Estimation","stem":"Historical data suggests that configuring one standard workstation costs $80. A team uses this rate to estimate 250 comparable configurations. What method and total are appropriate?","options":["Parametric estimating; $20,000","Earned value analysis; $20,000","Analogous estimating; $3,125","Bottom-up estimating; $330"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Parametric estimating applies a rate or model to quantities: 250 × $80 = $20,000. The rate must be suitable for the work being estimated.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M096","chapter":7,"topic":"Cost baseline","section":"Determining the Budget","stem":"A sponsor has approved a total budget but wants planned expenditure by period for later comparison with performance. Which output provides this time-phased reference for project cost control?","options":["Cost baseline","Product backlog","Stakeholder register","Issue log"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The cost baseline distributes approved budget over time. A total alone does not show when expenditure or value is planned.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M097","chapter":7,"topic":"Earned value","section":"Earned Value Management","stem":"A work package has a budget of $40,000 and is assessed as 25% complete. Actual spending is $15,000. Using budgeted value of work performed, what is its earned value?","options":["$10,000","$40,000","$15,000","$25,000"],"correct":0,"explanation":"EV is the budgeted value of completed work: .25 × $40,000 = $10,000. Actual spending is AC and does not determine the amount of value earned.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M098","chapter":7,"topic":"Cost variance","section":"Earned Value Management","stem":"At a status date, EV is $60,000 and AC is $75,000. The manager wants to know whether completed work cost more than its budgeted value. What is CV and its interpretation?","options":["-$15,000; ahead of schedule","+$135,000; over budget","+$15,000; under budget","-$15,000; over budget for work performed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"CV = EV - AC = -$15,000. A negative cost variance indicates cost overrun relative to the budgeted value of completed work, not a schedule condition.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M099","chapter":7,"topic":"Schedule variance","section":"Earned Value Management","stem":"At the same status date, EV is $60,000 and PV is $80,000. What does the EVM schedule variance indicate, without treating its monetary units as a direct count of calendar days?","options":["SV cannot be calculated without actual costs.","SV is -$20,000; less work is complete than planned.","SV is -20 days; the finish date is exactly 20 days late.","SV is +$20,000; more work is complete than planned."],"correct":1,"explanation":"SV = EV - PV = -$20,000. It expresses a value-based schedule shortfall; it does not directly report the number of days the project is late.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M100","chapter":7,"topic":"Cost performance index","section":"Earned Value Management","stem":"A team has earned $48,000 of budgeted work while spending $60,000. Which CPI and interpretation should the manager report when evaluating cost efficiency at this status date?","options":["1.25; $1.25 of budgeted work per dollar spent","0.80; $0.80 of budgeted work per dollar spent","1.25; the project must be ahead of schedule","0.80; 80% of the entire project is complete"],"correct":1,"explanation":"CPI = EV/AC = 48,000/60,000 = .80. This measures cost efficiency, not overall percentage complete or schedule status.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M101","chapter":7,"topic":"Schedule performance index","section":"Earned Value Management","stem":"A project has EV of $90,000, PV of $100,000, and AC of $85,000. Which SPI correctly measures progress against planned work, independently of cost efficiency?","options":["0.90","1.11","1.06","0.85"],"correct":0,"explanation":"SPI = EV/PV = .90, indicating less earned work than planned. EV/AC would calculate CPI, a different indicator.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M102","chapter":7,"topic":"Estimate at completion","section":"Earned Value Management","stem":"BAC is $200,000 and CPI is 0.80. Assume current cost efficiency will persist for the remaining work. Using EAC = BAC/CPI, what total cost forecast follows?","options":["$200,000","$250,000","$40,000","$160,000"],"correct":1,"explanation":"EAC = 200,000/.80 = $250,000. The formula depends on the stated assumption that current cost efficiency continues; it is not universally appropriate.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M103","chapter":7,"topic":"Contingency vs. management reserves","section":"Basic Principles of Cost Management","stem":"A manager distinguishes a budget allowance for identified risks from money held for unforeseen work within scope. Which reserve is associated with identified risks and is generally included in the cost baseline?","options":["Sunk-cost reserve","Management reserve","Contingency reserve","Profit margin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Contingency reserves address identified risks or known unknowns and are generally included in the baseline. Management reserves address unknown unknowns and are outside the cost baseline.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M104","chapter":8,"topic":"Quality vs. grade","section":"What Is Project Quality Management?","stem":"A basic application has few features but reliably meets every agreed requirement. A premium application has many features but fails frequently. Which conclusion best separates quality from grade?","options":["Lower-grade products must have unacceptable quality.","The basic application can have high quality despite its lower grade.","More features automatically mean higher quality.","Quality depends only on the number of features."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Quality concerns meeting requirements and fitness for use. Grade relates to category or feature level; a simple product can be reliable and fully fit for its purpose.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M105","chapter":8,"topic":"Plan quality","section":"Planning Quality Management","stem":"Before testing starts, a team agrees response-time targets, defect thresholds, and how compliance will be demonstrated. Which quality-management activity is most directly represented by these decisions?","options":["Controlling quality after delivery","Validating scope only","Planning quality management","Closing procurement"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Planning quality identifies relevant standards and how to satisfy them. Measuring completed results against those standards comes later in control quality.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M106","chapter":8,"topic":"Manage quality","section":"Managing Quality","stem":"An audit finds that teams skip an agreed peer-review process. The manager improves the development process to prevent defects, rather than merely retesting the latest build. Which focus best describes this action?","options":["Reducing product scope without approval","Measuring only the latest product's defects","Accepting finished deliverables from the customer","Managing quality through process improvement"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Managing quality translates quality plans into activities and improves processes. Control quality is more directly concerned with checking particular outputs.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M107","chapter":8,"topic":"Control quality","section":"Controlling Quality","stem":"Testers run the completed module against specified performance criteria, record defects, and verify corrections. Which quality-management process most directly checks these particular work results?","options":["Developing the project charter","Planning scope management","Controlling quality","Strategic project selection"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Control quality monitors specific results for compliance with standards. It differs from setting the standards or improving the underlying processes.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M108","chapter":8,"topic":"Pareto chart","section":"Tools and Techniques for Quality Control","stem":"A help desk categorizes incidents and finds a few causes account for most complaints. Which chart best helps prioritize these categories from the largest contributor downward?","options":["Scatter diagram","Project network diagram","Pareto chart","Resource histogram only"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A Pareto chart orders categories by frequency or impact to highlight the vital few contributors. A scatter diagram explores relationships between variables.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M109","chapter":8,"topic":"Cause-and-effect diagram","section":"Tools and Techniques for Quality Control","stem":"A team explores why deployments fail by grouping potential causes under people, methods, tools, and environment. Which quality tool is best suited to organizing these possible root causes?","options":["Cost baseline","Gantt chart","Cause-and-effect diagram","Stakeholder register"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A cause-and-effect or fishbone diagram organizes possible causes of a problem. It supports root-cause exploration, rather than plotting activities over time.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M110","chapter":8,"topic":"Control charts","section":"Tools and Techniques for Quality Control","stem":"A team records process performance in time order and compares it with calculated upper and lower control limits to identify unusual variation. Which tool serves this purpose?","options":["Pareto chart","WBS dictionary","Control chart","Responsibility matrix"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Control charts track process variation over time and help distinguish stable behavior from special causes. Control limits are not simply the customer's specification limits.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M111","chapter":8,"topic":"Scatter diagrams","section":"Tools and Techniques for Quality Control","stem":"Analysts want to examine whether larger code changes tend to coincide with more defects. They plot paired observations of change size and defect count. Which tool is being used?","options":["Flowchart","Checksheet only","Milestone chart","Scatter diagram"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A scatter diagram explores a possible relationship between two variables. An observed association alone does not prove a causal relationship.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M112","chapter":8,"topic":"Six Sigma","section":"Six Sigma","stem":"A team adopts a structured improvement method to reduce variation and defects in an existing support process. Which sequence correctly expands the DMAIC improvement cycle used in Six Sigma?","options":["Develop, manage, approve, inspect, close","Define, measure, analyze, improve, control","Design, measure, accept, implement, certify","Define, monitor, authorize, integrate, close"],"correct":1,"explanation":"DMAIC means define, measure, analyze, improve, and control. It is a process-improvement cycle, not the five project management process groups.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M113","chapter":8,"topic":"Prevention costs","section":"The Cost of Quality","stem":"A company pays for training and improved development practices to prevent defects before they occur. How should these expenditures be classified within the cost of quality?","options":["Internal failure costs","Prevention costs","External failure costs","Sunk benefits"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Prevention costs aim to avoid defects. Appraisal costs evaluate quality; failure costs arise from defects discovered before or after delivery.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M114","chapter":8,"topic":"Appraisal costs","section":"The Cost of Quality","stem":"A team spends money on inspections and testing to determine whether outputs meet requirements. Which cost-of-quality category best describes these evaluation activities, assuming no rework is included?","options":["Appraisal costs","External failure costs","Warranty costs only","Prevention costs"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Appraisal costs cover evaluating conformance, such as inspection and testing. Rework and customer warranty claims belong to failure-related categories.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M115","chapter":8,"topic":"Failure costs","section":"The Cost of Quality","stem":"A defect is found and corrected before shipment; a different defect produces warranty claims after delivery. How should the two costs be classified, respectively?","options":["Appraisal and prevention","Prevention and appraisal","External failure and prevention","Internal failure and external failure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Internal failure costs arise from defects before delivery. External failure costs arise after delivery, such as warranty work and customer-related consequences.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M116","chapter":8,"topic":"Testing levels","section":"Testing","stem":"A single component passes its own tests, but problems appear when it exchanges data with another component. Which testing level most directly targets the interaction now failing?","options":["Integration testing","Unit testing only","Cost-baseline validation","Project portfolio review"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Integration testing examines combined components and their interfaces. Unit testing focuses on individual components; passing it does not guarantee correct interactions.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M117","chapter":9,"topic":"Herzberg","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A company improves salaries and office conditions, reducing complaints, yet employees still lack enthusiasm for their work. According to Herzberg, which additional factor is more directly a motivator?","options":["Basic workplace conditions alone","Removing all challenging assignments","Achievement and meaningful responsibility","Administrative policies alone"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Herzberg distinguishes hygiene factors, which reduce dissatisfaction, from motivators such as achievement, responsibility, recognition, and growth.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M118","chapter":9,"topic":"Maslow","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A manager uses Maslow's hierarchy to consider an employee's desire for achievement, recognition, and respect. Which need level is most directly associated with these concerns?","options":["Safety","Basic survival","Esteem","Physiological"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Esteem concerns recognition, respect, and achievement. Physiological and safety needs concern more basic conditions; self-actualization involves realizing one's potential.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M119","chapter":9,"topic":"McClelland","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A team member enjoys challenging but achievable goals and wants clear feedback on personal results. Which acquired need in McClelland's theory is most directly evident?","options":["Need for hygiene","Need for affiliation only","Need for security","Need for achievement"],"correct":3,"explanation":"McClelland identifies achievement, affiliation, and power as acquired needs. Challenging goals and performance feedback particularly fit achievement.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M120","chapter":9,"topic":"Theory X","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A supervisor assumes workers inherently dislike work and must be closely controlled or threatened to perform. Which management assumption does this illustrate in McGregor's theory?","options":["Theory Z","Theory Y","Self-actualization","Theory X"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Theory X assumes people dislike work and need control. Theory Y assumes people can exercise self-direction and find work natural under appropriate conditions.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M121","chapter":9,"topic":"Theory Y","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A manager gives a capable team meaningful responsibility, believing that committed people can direct themselves and seek responsibility. Which assumption is most consistent with this management approach?","options":["Hygiene theory as a reporting structure","Theory X","Theory Y","Coercion as the only source of effort"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Theory Y supports self-direction and responsibility under suitable conditions. It does not mean that goals, coordination, or management become unnecessary.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M122","chapter":9,"topic":"Expert power","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"Developers follow an architect's advice because they respect her deep technical knowledge, although she has no formal authority over their employment. What is the main power base here?","options":["Reward power","Coercive power","Legitimate power","Expert power"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Expert power comes from knowledge or skill. Legitimate power comes from a formal position; coercive and reward power rely on penalties and benefits.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M123","chapter":9,"topic":"Referent power","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A team follows a coordinator because members admire and identify with him. He controls neither salaries nor formal job assignments. Which power base best describes this influence?","options":["Referent power","Coercive power","Legitimate power","Reward power"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Referent power arises from admiration and personal identification. The scenario does not establish authority, reward control, or the threat of punishment.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M124","chapter":9,"topic":"RACI","section":"Planning Resource Management","stem":"A responsibility matrix distinguishes people doing the work from the person answerable for its successful completion. In RACI, which letter identifies that ultimate accountability for the specified activity?","options":["A","C","R","I"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A means accountable. R means responsible for performing work, C consulted, and I informed. The distinction helps clarify ownership versus contribution.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M125","chapter":9,"topic":"Resource leveling","section":"Resource Loading and Leveling","stem":"One specialist is assigned to two full-time tasks on the same day. The manager adjusts task dates to resolve the over-allocation, accepting that the finish date may move. What technique is this?","options":["Fast tracking","Resource leveling","Scope validation","Gold plating"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Resource leveling resolves resource conflicts by adjusting the schedule and can extend project duration. Fast tracking overlaps activities and may worsen such a conflict.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M126","chapter":9,"topic":"Team development","section":"Developing the Project Team","stem":"Team members start challenging one another's roles and working methods after an initially polite orientation. According to Tuckman's model, which stage most directly describes this conflict-prone period?","options":["Storming","Adjourning","Performing","Forming"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Storming involves conflict and adjustment. Forming is initial orientation; norming develops shared practices; performing is effective work; adjourning is separation.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M127","chapter":9,"topic":"Conflict resolution","section":"Managing the Project Team","stem":"Two specialists disagree about an interface. The manager helps them examine the underlying needs and jointly develop a solution that satisfies both, rather than splitting the difference. Which approach is this?","options":["Collaborating or problem solving","Withdrawing","Forcing","Smoothing only"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Collaboration confronts the underlying problem to seek a mutually satisfactory solution. Compromise gives up something on both sides; smoothing downplays differences.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M128","chapter":9,"topic":"Emotional intelligence","section":"Keys to Managing and Leading People","stem":"A manager notices personal frustration during a tense meeting, regulates the reaction, and recognizes anxiety in colleagues before responding constructively. Which capability is most directly demonstrated?","options":["Parametric estimating","Critical path analysis","Coercive authority","Emotional intelligence"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Emotional intelligence involves recognizing and managing one's own emotions and understanding others' emotions. It supports constructive leadership and interaction.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M129","chapter":9,"topic":"Acquiring vs. developing","section":"Acquiring Resources","stem":"The project manager negotiates for a specialist to join the team. Later, that specialist attends training to improve collaboration and skills. Which pair of processes best describes these actions in order?","options":["Acquiring resources, then developing the team","Developing the team, then identifying stakeholders","Managing procurement, then validating scope","Controlling scope, then closing the project"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Acquiring resources obtains people or physical resources. Developing the team improves competencies, interaction, and team effectiveness after resources are available.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M130","chapter":10,"topic":"Communication channels","section":"Understanding Communication Channels","stem":"A six-person team adds two members. Using n(n−1)/2 potential two-way communication channels, how many channels does the eight-person team have, and how many were added?","options":["56 total; 26 added","28 total; 15 added","28 total; 13 added","16 total; 4 added"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Eight people yield 8×7/2 = 28 channels. Six yielded 6×5/2 = 15, so 13 channels were added. The formula counts unordered pairs.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M131","chapter":10,"topic":"Communications plan","section":"Planning Communications Management","stem":"Stakeholders complain that they receive either too much detail or updates too late. Which plan should specify information needs, recipients, methods, timing, and responsibility for distributing project information?","options":["Activity duration estimate","Communications management plan","Procurement statement of work","Scope baseline"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The communications plan establishes who needs what information, when, how, and from whom. It should be tailored to stakeholder needs.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M132","chapter":10,"topic":"Interactive communication","section":"Managing Communications","stem":"Two teams must resolve a complex misunderstanding quickly through questions and immediate feedback. Which communication method best supports a shared understanding during the exchange?","options":["Posting a document without notifying anyone","A one-way bulk newsletter","Archiving a report for later retrieval","Interactive discussion"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Interactive communication permits immediate exchange and feedback. Push and pull methods can be useful but are less suited to resolving an urgent complex misunderstanding alone.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M133","chapter":10,"topic":"Push communication","section":"Managing Communications","stem":"A manager emails a weekly status report directly to a defined distribution list. Recipients receive the message, but the manager cannot assume they understood it. What method is illustrated?","options":["Pull communication","No communication unless everyone replies","Interactive communication by definition","Push communication"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Push sends information to recipients. Delivery does not guarantee reading or understanding; a one-way email is not automatically an interactive exchange.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M134","chapter":10,"topic":"Pull communication","section":"Managing Communications","stem":"A large project stores detailed reference materials in a portal so interested users can retrieve them when needed. Which communication method best describes this access model?","options":["Push communication","Coercive communication","Interactive conversation","Pull communication"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Pull communication makes information available for recipients to access. It is suitable for large bodies of information but requires users to know where and how to retrieve it.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M135","chapter":10,"topic":"Audience needs","section":"Keys to Good Communications","stem":"An executive wants decisions and business impact, while developers need technical detail. What reporting approach best addresses both without assuming one identical document suits every audience?","options":["Avoid executive reports until all technical work is finished.","Send the full technical log to everyone.","Send only financial totals to every stakeholder.","Tailor content and detail to each audience's needs."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Effective communication considers individual and group needs. The same raw information may need different presentation and level of detail for different recipients.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M136","chapter":10,"topic":"Bad news","section":"Keys to Good Communications","stem":"The team discovers a serious delay. The manager wants to preserve trust and enable a decision. Which approach best supports effective communication of this unwelcome development?","options":["Send only optimistic metrics unrelated to the delay.","Hide the delay until there is no possible recovery.","Wait for the sponsor to discover the issue independently.","Communicate promptly with evidence, impacts, and options."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Timely, accurate communication of bad news enables action. Concealment reduces trust and deprives stakeholders of opportunities to help resolve the problem.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M137","chapter":10,"topic":"Active listening","section":"Keys to Good Communications","stem":"During a requirements discussion, the manager summarizes what the user said and asks whether the summary is accurate before proposing a solution. Which practice is most directly demonstrated?","options":["Resource crashing","Scope acceptance","One-way reporting","Active listening and checking understanding"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paraphrasing and confirming meaning reduces misunderstanding. Listening is an active part of communication, not merely waiting to speak.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M138","chapter":10,"topic":"Status vs. progress","section":"Reporting Performance","stem":"A report describes where the project stands today, while another describes work completed during the last fortnight. Which pair of report types best matches these purposes respectively?","options":["Progress report and forecast","Status report and progress report","Forecast and status report","Risk register and business case"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Status describes the position at a point in time. Progress describes accomplishments over a period; a forecast estimates future performance.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M139","chapter":10,"topic":"Forecasts","section":"Reporting Performance","stem":"Using current trends, a manager predicts the likely completion date and total cost rather than merely reporting historical results. Which type of performance information is being produced?","options":["Requirements traceability matrix","Stakeholder register","Acceptance certificate","Forecast"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A forecast predicts future conditions or outcomes using available information. A status report instead describes the current position.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M140","chapter":10,"topic":"Effective meetings","section":"Suggestions for Improving Project Communications","stem":"A weekly meeting runs long and produces no decisions or follow-up. Which change most directly improves its usefulness for participants and the project?","options":["Remove all time limits and avoid assigning action owners.","Replace every discussion with an unstructured status monologue.","Invite everyone regardless of relevance.","Use a clear purpose, agenda, and recorded actions with owners."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Effective meetings need a purpose, appropriate participants, structure, and follow-through. More attendance or more time does not by itself improve results.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M141","chapter":10,"topic":"Monitoring communications","section":"Controlling Communications","stem":"Reports are distributed exactly as planned, but stakeholders still cannot make needed decisions. What should the manager do when assessing whether project communications are effective?","options":["Stop collecting feedback because the plan was approved.","Treat distribution as proof that communication succeeded.","Increase report length without investigating the problem.","Check whether information needs are met and adapt the approach."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Monitoring or controlling communications considers whether information flows effectively and meets needs. Sending information is an activity, not proof of understanding or usefulness.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M142","chapter":10,"topic":"Lessons learned","section":"Suggestions for Improving Project Communications","stem":"A team wants future projects to benefit from both its successful practices and its mistakes. Which communication artifact best records these insights rather than merely listing current tasks?","options":["Daily attendance register","Supplier invoice","Lessons-learned report","Activity resource calendar"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Lessons learned capture experience that can improve future work. They should describe useful insights and context, not simply record who attended or which task was scheduled.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M143","chapter":11,"topic":"Threats and opportunities","section":"The Importance of Project Risk Management","stem":"A supplier may deliver late, while a new automation technique might reduce testing time. Both are uncertain. How should the project manager treat them in risk management?","options":["Treat the opportunity as a guaranteed benefit.","Track only the possible delay as a risk.","Consider both the threat and the opportunity.","Record both as issues that have already occurred."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Risk includes uncertain events with negative or positive effects. A realized event is an issue; an opportunity remains uncertain until it occurs.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M144","chapter":11,"topic":"Risk register","section":"Identifying Risks","stem":"The team records potential events, causes, owners, likelihood, impacts, and responses in a shared document that evolves during delivery. What artifact is this?","options":["WBS dictionary","Risk register","Supplier scorecard","Scope statement"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The risk register captures identified risks and related analysis and response information. It is updated throughout the project rather than fixed at initiation.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M145","chapter":11,"topic":"Qualitative analysis","section":"Performing Qualitative Risk Analysis","stem":"A workshop rates risks as low, medium, or high using likelihood and impact categories, then prioritizes attention. Which analysis is most directly being performed?","options":["Earned value analysis","Simple payback analysis","Qualitative risk analysis","Quantitative simulation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Qualitative analysis prioritizes risks using assessments such as probability and impact scales. Quantitative analysis models numerical effects on project objectives.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M146","chapter":11,"topic":"Expected monetary value","section":"Performing Quantitative Risk Analysis","stem":"A risk has a 20% probability of causing a $50,000 loss. For a simple expected monetary value calculation with no other outcomes, what is the expected loss?","options":["$40,000","$50,000","$10,000","$250,000"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Expected loss = .20 × $50,000 = $10,000, or EMV of -$10,000 when losses use negative signs. It is an average expectation, not the certain realized loss.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M147","chapter":11,"topic":"Monte Carlo simulation","section":"Performing Quantitative Risk Analysis","stem":"A manager uses probability distributions for uncertain durations and repeatedly simulates possible project outcomes to estimate the chance of finishing by a target date. Which technique is this?","options":["A stakeholder power grid","A single deterministic schedule calculation","A qualitative checklist only","Monte Carlo simulation"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Monte Carlo simulation samples uncertain inputs repeatedly to model possible outcomes. It can estimate probabilities of schedule or cost targets.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M148","chapter":11,"topic":"Avoidance","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"A project removes a risky experimental component from its approved approach and chooses a proven design, eliminating a specific threat associated with that component. Which response is most directly illustrated?","options":["Risk acceptance","Risk transfer","Risk exploitation","Risk avoidance"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Avoidance changes the approach to eliminate a threat. Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact while the threat remains; transfer shifts responsibility or consequences to another party.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M149","chapter":11,"topic":"Mitigation","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"The team adds compatibility tests early to lower the probability of an integration failure. It still uses the same technology and retains responsibility. Which threat response best describes this action?","options":["Risk transfer","Risk acceptance without action","Risk mitigation","Risk exploitation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Mitigation reduces a threat's probability or impact. The risk has not been removed entirely or shifted to another party.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M150","chapter":11,"topic":"Transfer","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"An organization purchases insurance against a specified financial loss, shifting covered financial consequences to an insurer. Which risk response does this represent, without assuming the underlying event cannot happen?","options":["Risk avoidance","Risk exploitation","Risk enhancement","Risk transference"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Transfer shifts consequences and management responsibility through mechanisms such as insurance. It does not eliminate the event or every residual responsibility.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M151","chapter":11,"topic":"Exploitation","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"A rare opportunity could deliver an early release. The manager assigns the strongest available resources specifically to make the opportunity occur, rather than simply increasing its likelihood slightly. Which positive-risk response is intended?","options":["Exploitation","Transference of a threat","Mitigation","Passive acceptance"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Exploitation aims to ensure a positive opportunity is realized. Enhancement increases its probability or impact without necessarily trying to make it certain.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M152","chapter":11,"topic":"Sharing","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"A company forms a partnership with another firm that has the capabilities needed to capture a market opportunity. They agree to share benefits and responsibility. Which positive-risk strategy is illustrated?","options":["Sharing","Threat mitigation only","Coercion","Avoidance"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Sharing allocates opportunity ownership to a party or partnership best positioned to realize it. It is a positive-risk response, distinct from transferring a threat.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M153","chapter":11,"topic":"Contingency vs. fallback","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"A team prepares a response to use if an identified risk occurs, plus a backup response if that first response fails. What are the two plans called respectively?","options":["Contingency plan and fallback plan","Risk register and resource histogram","Baseline and scope statement","Fallback plan and project charter"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A contingency plan is activated for an identified event. A fallback plan is used if the initial response proves ineffective.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M154","chapter":11,"topic":"Delphi technique","section":"Identifying Risks","stem":"Experts provide anonymous risk opinions through repeated questionnaires, with summarized feedback between rounds. The team wants to reduce dominance by a vocal participant. Which technique is being used?","options":["Open brainstorming only","Pareto analysis","Fast tracking","Delphi technique"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Delphi seeks expert input through structured, often anonymous rounds. Anonymity and feedback help reduce social pressure and individual dominance.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M155","chapter":11,"topic":"Residual vs. secondary risk","section":"Planning Risk Responses","stem":"A mitigation leaves some of the original threat unresolved and also introduces a new dependency risk. What are these risks called respectively?","options":["Residual risk and secondary risk","Issue and acceptance criterion","Secondary risk and residual risk","Opportunity and sunk cost"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Residual risk remains after a response. Secondary risk is newly created by implementing the response; both need appropriate attention.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M156","chapter":12,"topic":"Make-or-buy analysis","section":"Planning Procurement Management","stem":"An organization compares building an integration tool internally with purchasing an outside service, considering costs and capability. What analysis is most directly being performed before deciding how to obtain the result?","options":["Scope validation","Make-or-buy analysis","Team-development assessment","Earned value analysis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Make-or-buy analysis compares internal provision and external procurement. It can include strategic and capability considerations as well as direct costs.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M157","chapter":12,"topic":"Fixed-price contracts","section":"Types of Contracts","stem":"A buyer has a clear specification and agrees a fixed total price for the defined work. If the seller's own costs rise without an approved scope change, who normally bears that cost-overrun risk?","options":["Neither party because fixed price removes all risk","The project sponsor personally","The buyer automatically","The seller"],"correct":3,"explanation":"For the defined scope, a fixed-price arrangement generally places more cost-overrun risk on the seller. Changes and contract terms can alter responsibilities.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M158","chapter":12,"topic":"Cost-reimbursable contracts","section":"Types of Contracts","stem":"The scope is uncertain and the buyer agrees to reimburse allowable actual costs plus a fee. Compared with a firm fixed price for the same work, which party generally carries more cost uncertainty?","options":["The seller exclusively","Neither party has cost exposure.","The buyer","The buyer carries none because costs are documented."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Cost-reimbursable arrangements place more actual-cost risk on the buyer. Cost visibility and controls help but do not eliminate uncertainty.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M159","chapter":12,"topic":"Time and material","section":"Types of Contracts","stem":"Consultants charge agreed hourly rates plus specified material costs, while the total number of hours is uncertain. Which contract form most directly describes the arrangement?","options":["Cost-free partnership","Firm fixed price for the entire job","Time and material","Purchase by final outcome only"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Time-and-material contracts combine fixed rates with uncertain quantities. A fixed hourly rate does not make the total project price fixed.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M160","chapter":12,"topic":"Procurement statement of work","section":"Planning Procurement Management","stem":"Prospective suppliers need enough detail about the work to decide whether they can perform it and prepare a suitable price. Which document should describe the contracted work and deliverables?","options":["Earned value chart","Employee performance review","Project communications log","Procurement statement of work"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A procurement SOW defines the work to be obtained in enough detail for potential sellers. It supports a common understanding of the procurement's scope.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M161","chapter":12,"topic":"Request for proposal","section":"Planning Procurement Management","stem":"A buyer wants suppliers to propose how they would solve a business problem, including approach and capability, rather than quote an identical commodity. Which request is most appropriate?","options":["Project closure notice","Request for proposal","Request for a final invoice","Routine acceptance certificate"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An RFP solicits proposed solutions and approaches. A request for quotation is more appropriate when requirements are clear and a price quote is central.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M162","chapter":12,"topic":"Request for quotation","section":"Planning Procurement Management","stem":"A buyer needs a specified quantity of identical standard devices and primarily wants comparable prices for clearly defined items. Which procurement document is most appropriate?","options":["Request for a project charter","Request for a retrospective","Stakeholder engagement plan","Request for quotation"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An RFQ is useful for well-defined products or services where price comparison is central. An RFP invites broader solution proposals.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M163","chapter":12,"topic":"Source selection","section":"Conducting Procurements","stem":"A selection team agrees technical, management, and cost criteria before evaluating supplier proposals. What is the main benefit of applying this defined evaluation model consistently?","options":["A guarantee that the cheapest bid will always win","A more transparent and comparable basis for selection","Removal of the need to examine supplier capability","Proof that every proposal meets all requirements"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Defined criteria promote consistency and help evaluate trade-offs. They do not automatically favor the lowest price or guarantee qualification.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M164","chapter":12,"topic":"Bidder conferences","section":"Conducting Procurements","stem":"Several prospective suppliers have questions about ambiguous requirements. The buyer holds a shared clarification session before proposals are submitted. What is the principal purpose of this bidder conference?","options":["Formally close an already completed contract.","Give prospective sellers a common understanding of the procurement.","Allow one preferred seller to change the requirements privately.","Replace all written procurement requirements with informal promises."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Bidder conferences clarify requirements and help ensure a fair, shared understanding. Resulting clarifications should be handled consistently for the participating suppliers.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M165","chapter":12,"topic":"Controlling procurements","section":"Controlling Procurements","stem":"After contract award, the manager monitors seller performance, reviews proposed changes, and checks that agreed obligations are fulfilled. Which procurement process most directly covers this work?","options":["Identifying stakeholders only","Conducting project initiation","Controlling procurements","Strategic portfolio selection"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Controlling procurements manages the buyer-seller relationship and performance under the contract, including changes and closure activities. Award alone does not end procurement management.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M166","chapter":12,"topic":"Constructive change","section":"Controlling Procurements","stem":"A project employee informally tells a supplier to do extra work outside the agreed contract, believing a verbal request has no consequences. What is the most appropriate concern?","options":["Verbal directions can never affect contractual obligations.","The supplier automatically must do unlimited extra work for free.","The instruction may create a constructive change and contractual consequences.","Only written invoices can ever change project scope."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Informal directions can create constructive changes or disputes. Team members should respect contract authority and change procedures rather than assume informal requests are harmless.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M167","chapter":12,"topic":"Make-or-buy break-even","section":"Planning Procurement Management","stem":"Buying a tool costs $18,000 upfront plus $200 per month; renting costs $800 per month. Ignore all other costs and discounting. At how many months do the total costs become equal?","options":["90 months","30 months","18 months","22.5 months"],"correct":1,"explanation":"18,000 + 200m = 800m, so m = 18,000/600 = 30. Beyond 30 months buying is cheaper under these assumptions.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M168","chapter":12,"topic":"Contract closure","section":"Controlling Procurements","stem":"A vendor completes the work, but acceptance records and unresolved claims remain. Which action best supports proper procurement closure before treating the relationship as finished?","options":["Delete procurement records as soon as coding stops.","Assume the final code commit settles all claims.","Release every payment without checking contract conditions.","Verify completion and resolve or appropriately handle outstanding contractual matters."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Closure requires attention to completion, acceptance, records, and outstanding obligations or claims according to the contract. Technical completion alone is not sufficient.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M169","chapter":13,"topic":"Stakeholder identification","section":"Identifying Stakeholders","stem":"A new system affects employees, customers, a regulator, and a supplier. Only employees are currently listed. What should the manager do to establish a more complete stakeholder view?","options":["Limit the list to people employed by the organization.","Exclude regulators because they do not write code.","Include only groups expected to support the project.","Include relevant internal and external affected parties."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Stakeholders can be internal or external and supportive or opposed. Being affected or able to affect the project matters, not employee status alone.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M170","chapter":13,"topic":"Stakeholder register","section":"Identifying Stakeholders","stem":"A team records stakeholder identities, roles, contact information, interests, and classifications so later engagement planning has a foundation. Which artifact is being developed?","options":["WBS dictionary","Cost baseline","Stakeholder register","Risk breakdown structure"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A stakeholder register records identification, assessment, and classification information. It supports analysis and engagement planning.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M171","chapter":13,"topic":"Power-interest grid","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"An executive has both high decision power and high interest in the project's outcome. In a typical power-interest grid, what general engagement strategy is appropriate?","options":["Keep informed but avoid direct engagement","Treat as low priority because authority is high","Monitor with minimal effort","Manage closely"],"correct":3,"explanation":"High-power, high-interest stakeholders normally require close management. The grid guides attention but should still be adapted to the person's actual needs.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M172","chapter":13,"topic":"Keep satisfied","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A senior official has substantial power over the project but little interest in day-to-day detail. What broad power-interest strategy is usually appropriate for this stakeholder?","options":["Keep satisfied","Send every technical log without context","Assume no engagement is needed","Monitor with minimal effort only"],"correct":0,"explanation":"High power with low interest generally calls for keeping the stakeholder satisfied. Overwhelming them with unnecessary detail can be unhelpful.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M173","chapter":13,"topic":"Keep informed","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A user group has limited formal power but strong interest because the system affects daily work. What broad strategy fits its position on a power-interest grid?","options":["Manage only through procurement contracts","Assume it has high authority over every decision","Keep informed","Ignore entirely"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Low-power, high-interest stakeholders should generally be kept informed. Their knowledge and concerns can still be valuable despite limited formal authority.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M174","chapter":13,"topic":"Monitoring low-power stakeholders","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A peripheral group currently has little power and little interest in the project. Which broad strategy fits a power-interest grid while allowing circumstances to change later?","options":["Give it automatic approval authority.","Monitor with appropriate minimal effort.","Exclude it permanently from every future review.","Send all confidential project records daily."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Low-power, low-interest stakeholders typically need monitoring rather than intensive management. Their position may change, so this is not permanent exclusion.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M175","chapter":13,"topic":"Engagement assessment","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"The team compares each stakeholder's current engagement with the level needed for project success. Which tool is particularly suited to displaying these current and desired positions?","options":["Activity-on-arrow network","Earned value chart","Resource histogram","Stakeholder engagement assessment matrix"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An engagement assessment matrix compares current and desired engagement levels, helping identify gaps and targeted actions.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M176","chapter":13,"topic":"Resistant vs. unaware","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A department understands the proposed change but actively opposes it because of expected disruption. Which engagement classification best describes the department, rather than one that does not yet know about the project?","options":["Unaware","Leading","Supportive","Resistant"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Resistant stakeholders know about the project but oppose it. Unaware stakeholders lack awareness; supportive and leading stakeholders have different levels of positive engagement.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M177","chapter":13,"topic":"Managing engagement","section":"Managing Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A manager meets concerned users, clarifies expectations, and works through objections so they can contribute constructively. Which process most directly describes this active interaction?","options":["Managing stakeholder engagement","Performing portfolio selection","Conducting product-only quality tests","Creating the original cost baseline"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Managing engagement involves communication and interaction to address expectations and issues. It goes beyond merely identifying stakeholders or sending reports.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M178","chapter":13,"topic":"Issue log","section":"Managing Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A stakeholder raises an unresolved access problem that is already occurring. The manager records its owner, status, and resolution action. Which artifact most directly tracks this current matter?","options":["Procurement pricing schedule","Risk register only as an uncertain future event","Issue log","Product vision statement"],"correct":2,"explanation":"An issue log tracks current problems and their resolution. A risk register focuses on uncertainty; a realized problem needs issue handling.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M179","chapter":13,"topic":"Monitoring engagement","section":"Monitoring Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"A previously supportive director stops attending reviews after a reorganization. The manager reassesses engagement and changes the approach. Which process is most directly illustrated?","options":["Freezing the stakeholder plan permanently","Monitoring stakeholder engagement","Scope validation","Closing the project automatically"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Monitoring engagement assesses relationship and engagement changes and adjusts strategies. A stakeholder assessment is not a once-only activity.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M180","chapter":13,"topic":"Sensitive information","section":"Planning Stakeholder Engagement","stem":"An engagement plan includes candid assessments of individual influence and resistance. The team considers posting the entire plan publicly. Which handling is most appropriate for this sensitive analysis?","options":["Give all outside suppliers unrestricted access by default.","Share sensitive assessments only with appropriate authorized recipients.","Publish every personal assessment regardless of purpose.","Delete all stakeholder analysis to avoid any sensitivity."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Some stakeholder analysis is sensitive and should be handled carefully. Appropriate access preserves useful planning without unnecessary disclosure.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"M181","chapter":13,"topic":"Agile stakeholder involvement","section":"Considerations for Agile/Adaptive Environments","stem":"An agile team delivers frequent increments and needs quick feedback to reprioritize future work. Which stakeholder approach best supports this adaptive setting?","options":["Wait until final delivery for all customer feedback.","Treat the initial backlog as permanently fixed.","Frequent collaboration and timely decisions from relevant stakeholders.","Replace the product owner with an annual status report."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Adaptive work benefits from frequent stakeholder involvement and quick feedback. Delaying feedback undermines the ability to adjust priorities and deliver value.","type":"mcq"},{"id":"T194","chapter":7,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"A CPI below 1 indicates favorable cost efficiency.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"CPI = EV/AC. Below 1 means less budgeted work value is earned than money spent.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T195","chapter":7,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Sunk costs should by themselves justify continuing an uneconomic project.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Past unrecoverable spending is not a future benefit. Forward decisions should consider relevant future costs and benefits.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T196","chapter":8,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"A product with fewer features can still have high quality.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Grade and quality differ. A simple product can meet requirements and be fit for use.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T197","chapter":8,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Warranty repair after delivery is normally a prevention cost.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"It is an external failure cost because the defect affected a delivered product.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T198","chapter":9,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Resource leveling can increase project duration.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Resolving over-allocation may shift activities and move the finish date.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T199","chapter":9,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Expert power requires formal authority over employee salaries.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Expert power comes from knowledge or skill, not control over employment rewards.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T200","chapter":10,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"An eight-person team has 28 potential two-way communication channels.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"8×7/2 = 28 distinct pairs.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T201","chapter":10,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Sending an email proves the recipients understood its content.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Push delivery does not guarantee reading or understanding; feedback may be needed.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T202","chapter":11,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"All project risks necessarily have negative effects.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Risk includes opportunities with potentially positive effects as well as threats.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T203","chapter":11,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"A secondary risk is a new risk introduced by a risk response.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A residual risk instead remains after the response.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T204","chapter":12,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"A fixed hourly consulting rate necessarily fixes the total contract price.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Total time-and-material cost also depends on hours and materials used.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T205","chapter":12,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Fixed-price contracts generally place defined-scope cost-overrun risk on the seller.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"This general allocation depends on contract terms and excludes approved changes to the work.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T206","chapter":13,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Only people who support a project should appear in the stakeholder register.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Opponents and other affected parties are also stakeholders.","type":"tf"},{"id":"T207","chapter":13,"topic":"True / false","section":"Chapter review","stem":"Stakeholder engagement should be reassessed as the project and organization change.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Power, interest, and engagement can change; monitoring supports adjustments.","type":"tf"}],"midtermEdition":"Schwalbe, Information Technology Project Management, ninth edition"};
